WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.480
A tracking station measures a satellite and radios down six numbers: three for its position, and three for its velocity.

00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:09.895
Here they are.

00:00:09.895 --> 00:00:13.095
Can you tell what this orbit looks like?

00:00:13.095 --> 00:00:15.095
Is it round or stretched?

00:00:15.095 --> 00:00:16.295
Tilted or flat?

00:00:16.295 --> 00:00:19.095
Where is the satellite in its loop?

00:00:19.395 --> 00:00:23.995
Worse, as the satellite moves, all six numbers keep changing.

00:00:24.328 --> 00:00:29.368
Now here is the same orbit, described by a different set of six numbers.

00:00:29.661 --> 00:00:31.531
Five of them never change.

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:35.128
Only the last one moves, like the hand of a clock.

00:00:35.128 --> 00:00:38.581
And every one of them has a picture you can see.

00:00:38.861 --> 00:00:45.101
This lesson is about those six numbers: what each one means, how to get them from a measurement, and how to go back.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.020
First we need a frame to measure everything in.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.500
It's called the Earth centered inertial frame, or E C I.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.380
K points through the North Pole.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:58.580
I and J lie in the plane of the equator.

00:00:59.487 --> 00:01:02.319
But which way, exactly, does I point?

00:01:02.319 --> 00:01:05.687
It has to be a direction the stars agree on.

00:01:07.853 --> 00:01:09.827
Picture Earth going around the Sun.

00:01:09.827 --> 00:01:15.693
Earth's equator is tilted about twenty three and a half degrees to the plane of its orbit, the ecliptic.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:19.132
The two planes cross along one line.

00:01:19.132 --> 00:01:23.886
On the March equinox, that line points from the Earth straight at the Sun.

00:01:23.886 --> 00:01:27.740
That is the vernal equinox direction, and I points along it.

00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:34.567
J is ninety degrees from I in the equator, completing a right handed set with K.

00:01:34.953 --> 00:01:37.330
One more detail matters a lot.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:40.500
This frame does not spin with the Earth.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:47.473
The Earth turns underneath it once a day, while I, J and K stay fixed against the stars.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:52.215
Position and velocity together are called the state vector.

00:01:52.215 --> 00:01:55.300
Those six numbers completely determine the orbit.

00:01:55.663 --> 00:02:01.583
The classical orbital elements are a different six numbers that determine the same orbit.

00:02:01.929 --> 00:02:07.229
Two describe size and shape: the semi major axis a, and the eccentricity e.

00:02:07.229 --> 00:02:10.409
You already know these from the last chapter.

00:02:10.696 --> 00:02:19.216
Three describe orientation: how steeply the orbit plane is tilted, which way that tilt faces, and how the ellipse is turned within its plane.

00:02:19.629 --> 00:02:27.189
And one says where the satellite is at the moment of the measurement: the true anomaly at epoch, theta zero.

00:02:27.596 --> 00:02:29.928
Five constants and one clock.

00:02:29.928 --> 00:02:35.316
Next, we'll build the three orientation angles, one turn at a time.
